1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:07,175
A customer writes: the package never showed up. Your FAQ says: where is my order? Not one word in common.

2
00:00:07,625 --> 00:00:16,117
Keyword search misses it. Embeddings find it. In this video, you'll learn what an embedding is, what normalizing a vector means, and why it matters.

3
00:00:16,567 --> 00:00:20,110
Then we'll build a small FAQ search in Python, step by step.

4
00:00:21,660 --> 00:00:22,689
So, what is an embedding?

5
00:00:23,139 --> 00:00:29,339
An embedding is a list of numbers that captures what a text means. A list of numbers like this is called a vector.

6
00:00:29,789 --> 00:00:35,843
Texts with similar meaning get vectors that point in a similar direction, even when they use different words.

7
00:00:36,293 --> 00:00:46,545
They come from an embedding model. Anthropic doesn't make one; its docs point to Voyage AI. Voyage's latest models return one thousand and twenty-four numbers per text by default.

8
00:00:47,995 --> 00:00:53,078
Picture each vector as an arrow. Two things matter: its direction, and its length.

9
00:00:53,528 --> 00:00:59,252
The length, also called the L2 norm, is the square root of the sum of the squares of its numbers.

10
00:00:59,702 --> 00:01:08,163
Meaning lives in the direction. Cosine similarity compares only the direction: one means the same direction, zero means unrelated.

11
00:01:08,613 --> 00:01:15,628
Normalizing means dividing a vector by its length. The direction stays the same, but the length becomes exactly one.

12
00:01:16,078 --> 00:01:24,751
And here's the payoff: when every vector has length one, the simple dot product gives the same number as cosine similarity, and it's faster to compute.

13
00:01:26,201 --> 00:01:29,897
Careful: normalization means two different things in AI.

14
00:01:30,347 --> 00:01:36,675
For embeddings, it means scaling each vector to length one before you compare vectors. That's today's topic.

15
00:01:37,125 --> 00:01:48,565
Inside a language model, normalization layers like LayerNorm and RMS Norm rescale the numbers between layers, to keep training stable. They're built into the model. You never call them yourself.

16
00:01:50,015 --> 00:01:51,367
When should you use embeddings?

17
00:01:51,817 --> 00:01:59,435
To search by meaning, to find the right chunks for RAG, to spot duplicates, for recommendations, and to group similar texts.

18
00:01:59,885 --> 00:02:06,620
But for exact codes, IDs and rare names, keyword search is better. Real systems often combine both.

19
00:02:08,070 --> 00:02:13,628
Our use case: an FAQ search for Maple Mugs, the small online shop from my RAG video.

20
00:02:14,078 --> 00:02:19,794
With keyword search, the package never showed up shares zero words with where is my order. No match.

21
00:02:20,244 --> 00:02:26,047
With embeddings, both questions become vectors that point the same way, so the right answer comes first.

22
00:02:27,497 --> 00:02:30,959
Let's start with the math, in plain Python. No libraries.

23
00:02:31,409 --> 00:02:37,026
Length is the square root of the sum of squares. Normalize divides every number by that length.

24
00:02:37,476 --> 00:02:46,076
So a vector like three, four has length five. Normalized, it becomes zero point six, zero point eight: same direction, length one.

25
00:02:46,926 --> 00:02:48,826
Step two: compare vectors.

26
00:02:49,276 --> 00:02:57,482
The dot product multiplies the numbers pair by pair, and adds them up. Cosine divides that by both lengths, so only the direction counts.

27
00:02:57,932 --> 00:03:05,311
Now a tiny example with arrows of just two numbers. A points the same way as the query. B points elsewhere, but it's longer.

28
00:03:05,761 --> 00:03:07,538
Let's run it, and look at the numbers.

29
00:03:07,988 --> 00:03:16,390
Look: the raw dot product prefers B, only because B is longer. Cosine gets it right: A is a perfect match, one point zero.

30
00:03:17,240 --> 00:03:18,931
Step three: normalize.

31
00:03:19,381 --> 00:03:25,619
The second half of the output does the same comparison, but normalizes the query and both documents first.

32
00:03:26,069 --> 00:03:33,220
Every length is now one, and the dot product gives exactly the cosine values. No division needed when you compare.

33
00:03:33,670 --> 00:03:40,468
That's why embedding providers normalize their vectors. You store them once, and every comparison is a fast dot product.

34
00:03:41,818 --> 00:03:45,217
Step four: embed the FAQ with real embeddings.

35
00:03:45,667 --> 00:03:52,655
With the Voyage AI package installed, I create a client. It reads your Voyage API key from an environment variable.

36
00:03:55,284 --> 00:04:03,412
Embed sends the five FAQ questions, and gets back five vectors. Note input type: document. Questions you search with use query.

37
00:04:03,862 --> 00:04:07,053
The print line shows the size of each vector, and its length.

38
00:04:07,903 --> 00:04:09,836
Step five: search by meaning.

39
00:04:10,286 --> 00:04:17,980
I embed the customer's question, with input type query. Then I score every FAQ with a dot product, and print the top three.

40
00:04:18,430 --> 00:04:25,055
Why a dot product, not cosine? Voyage vectors already have length one, so the dot product is the cosine.

41
00:04:25,505 --> 00:04:27,217
Let's run it with our customer's question.

42
00:04:29,555 --> 00:04:35,068
Five vectors of one thousand and twenty-four numbers, each with length one. Already normalized.

43
00:04:35,518 --> 00:04:40,558
And the top match is where is my order, with zero words in common. That's search by meaning.

44
00:04:41,008 --> 00:04:46,852
Your exact scores will differ. The files also include an offline test, with no API key needed.

45
00:04:48,402 --> 00:04:48,990
Four tips.

46
00:04:49,440 --> 00:04:55,859
One: embed your documents and your queries with the same model. Vectors from different models can't be compared.

47
00:04:56,309 --> 00:05:03,766
Two: set input type. Voyage's docs say to use document for what you store, and query for what you search with. Don't leave it out.

48
00:05:04,216 --> 00:05:13,083
Three: if you shorten vectors you already stored, for example to two hundred and fifty-six numbers, their length drops below one. Normalize them again.

49
00:05:13,533 --> 00:05:18,594
And four: store normalized vectors, so every comparison is a fast dot product.

50
00:05:20,144 --> 00:05:20,798
Let's recap.

51
00:05:21,248 --> 00:05:25,239
An embedding is a vector of numbers, and its direction carries the meaning.

52
00:05:25,689 --> 00:05:29,279
Normalizing divides a vector by its length, so it has length one.

53
00:05:29,729 --> 00:05:35,485
And with length one everywhere, the dot product is the cosine similarity: simple, and fast.

54
00:05:36,935 --> 00:05:42,938
The code, a cheat sheet, and an offline test you can run without an API key are linked in the description.

55
00:05:43,388 --> 00:05:50,219
If this helped, like the video, and subscribe to Zero Cost AI for more free AI tutorials. See you in the next one!

